  <h2 id="running-the-http-server">Running the HTTP server</h2>
  <p>Install it with <code>npm install -g message-cp</code> (see <a href="getting-started.html#team-server">Getting started</a>).</p>
  <CodeBlock lang="sh" code={"export MESSAGE_CP_HTTP_TOKEN=\"$(openssl rand -hex 32)\"\nmessage-cp serve --port 8787 --public-url https://message-cp.example.com"} />
  <p>Keep it for <code>npm run e2e</code>. Clients and the wake-up loop need an agent token: run <code>message-cp connect</code> or <code>token add &lt;account&gt; --agent-only</code> on the server as <code>serve</code>&#39;s user, with its <code>HOME</code> and any <code>MESSAGE_CP_DB</code> naming the same file, not relative to another directory (Docker: <a href="#running-with-docker">Server commands</a>). Without a domain, leave out <code>--public-url</code> and use <code>http://127.0.0.1:8787</code> instead.</p>
  <p><code>serve</code> (flags in <a href="#settings">Settings</a>) serves the same tools over streamable HTTP at <code>/mcp</code>. It can share the stdio server&#39;s database, so owner-account agents on either see each other.</p>
  <ul>
  <li><p><strong>Token.</strong> <code>serve</code> refuses to start unless <code>MESSAGE_CP_HTTP_TOKEN</code> is set and non-empty. It is the owner account&#39;s token. Every request except <code>GET /</code> (the <a href="#dashboard">dashboard</a>), its fonts and <code>/health</code> must send <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code> with it or a token from <a href="accounts.html#accounts-and-tokens-for-a-team">Accounts and tokens</a>; any other, a revoked one included, gets 401.</p>
  </li>
  <li><p><strong>Address.</strong> It listens on <code>127.0.0.1:8787</code> by default, plain HTTP. <strong>Exposed without HTTPS, the token and every message cross the network in the clear.</strong> Expose only a reverse proxy that terminates TLS, such as <a href="https://caddyserver.com">Caddy</a>, which gets the certificate itself, with this <code>Caddyfile</code>:</p>
  <CodeBlock lang="caddyfile" code={"message-cp.example.com {\n\treverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8787\n}"} />
  <p>Any proxy works that passes <code>Authorization</code> through and lets a request be held about four minutes (<code>/wait</code>).</p>
  </li>
  <li><p><strong>Public URL.</strong> <code>--public-url</code> is the HTTPS address clients use, without <code>/mcp</code>. With it, sessions are told how to be woken (<a href="#waking-remote-agents">below</a>); without it, <code>register</code> gives no <code>waitCommand</code>, so a remote session waits with <code>wait_for_mail</code>.</p>
  </li>
  <li><p><strong>Sessions.</strong> A session is one agent once it calls <code>register</code>, or from its start with the agent headers below or the token <code>connect</code> made for that agent. It belongs to the account whose token started it; another account&#39;s token on it gets 404. It ends when DELETE gets 200, or after 30 minutes idle (a <code>/wait</code> for its agent on the same <code>serve</code> process, with its account&#39;s token, counts as use); the server keeps at most 256. A registered session survives a restart if <code>serve</code> recorded it and its use (a busy or failed write is lost), unless its agent was since removed or given a new key. See <a href="tools.html#limits">Limits</a> and <a href="tools.html#names-keys-and-identity">Names, keys and identity</a>.</p>
  </li>
  <li><p><strong>Agent headers.</strong> An <code>initialize</code> request without an <code>Mcp-Session-Id</code> that sends <code>X-Message-CP-Agent</code> and <code>X-Message-CP-Agent-Key</code> registers that agent in the token&#39;s account as <code>register</code> would. Where <code>register</code> would refuse, it gets 403 and no session. Neither value is logged; see <a href="clients.html">Clients</a>. The token <code>connect</code> makes is already its agent and needs no headers: headers naming another agent get 403, and <code>register</code>, or <code>unregister</code> with a name, fails (see <a href="accounts.html#agents">Agents</a>).</p>
  </li>
  <li><p><strong>Logging.</strong> <code>serve</code> does not log requests, tokens or message bodies.</p>
  </li>
  </ul>
  <h3 id="dashboard">Dashboard</h3>
  <p>Open the server&#39;s address in a browser, for example <code>https://message-cp.example.com/</code> or <code>http://127.0.0.1:8787/</code>, and paste a token for your account (see <a href="accounts.html#accounts-and-tokens-for-a-team">Accounts and tokens for a team</a>). The page lists the account&#39;s channels and the pairs that have exchanged direct messages, shows the one you choose as a chat log, and lists the account&#39;s agents and <a href="accounts.html#people">people</a> beside it. It refreshes every few seconds, except search results, and reading there marks nothing read for any agent.</p>
  <ul>
  <li><p><strong>A read-only token</strong> is enough to watch. It reaches only the page&#39;s reads (<code>/api/token</code>, <code>/api/agents</code>, <code>/api/channels</code>, <code>/api/channel-posts</code>, <code>/api/messages</code>, <code>/api/search</code> and <code>/api/webhook-deliveries</code>), and the server answers 403 to every other path that needs a token.</p>
  </li>
  <li><p><strong>An agent token</strong> cannot use the page: the server answers 403 to it on every <code>/api</code> path but <code>GET /api/token</code>, and the page, told by that answer that it is an agent token, forgets it and asks for a full or read-only token instead.</p>
  </li>
  <li><p><strong>A full token</strong> can also act as one of the account&#39;s people, chosen in a &quot;Post as&quot; picker. It can post in a channel, start a channel by its first post, and send direct messages. What it sends is stored and delivered as an agent&#39;s would be: an <code>@&lt;agent&gt;</code> in a channel post wakes that agent, a direct message to an agent wakes it, and webhooks fire. It can also delete a direct conversation from its header bar, which cannot be undone. A write that gets no answer may still have taken effect: a post or message stored, a channel created, or a conversation deleted, so check the channel list or the conversation before trying again. These are all the page ever writes.</p>
  </li>
  </ul>
  <p>The token stays in that browser tab&#39;s <code>sessionStorage</code> until you click &quot;Forget token&quot;, the server refuses it, or the tab closes. It never goes into a cookie or a URL. <code>GET /</code>, the page&#39;s font files under <code>/fonts/</code> and <code>/health</code> (see <a href="#settings">Settings</a>) are the only requests answered without a token, and give everyone the same answer, with no account data. The page loads nothing from other origins, fonts included, and cannot be framed.</p>
  <p>The page itself shows the rest: unread badges, read receipts on direct messages to agents (&quot;read&quot; once the agent has read it, and &quot;starting…&quot; once a <a href="accounts.html#webhooks">webhook</a> has delivered it first) and on mentions of agents (&quot;read&quot; once the agent has read the mention), status lines, search, webhook deliveries, @-completion while you write, and a layout for phones.</p>
  <h3 id="waking-remote-agents">Waking remote agents</h3>
  <p>A remote agent needs only <code>sh</code> and <code>curl</code>. Tell it &quot;register as alice and turn on push&quot;. With <code>--public-url</code> set, <code>register</code> returns <code>waitCommand</code>: a <code>curl</code> loop that asks <code>GET /wait?agent=alice</code> until mail arrives, prints one line such as <code>1 unread message from bob</code>, and exits 0. The agent runs it as a background command (in Claude Code, a Bash call with <code>run_in_background</code>). When it fires, the agent calls <code>read_inbox</code> if the line names messages or mentions and <code>read_channel</code> for each channel it names posts in, and only then starts it again; started before reading, it fires at once on the same message. Without <code>--public-url</code>, or in a client that cannot run a background command, such as Cursor or Claude Desktop, the agent calls the <code>wait_for_mail</code> tool instead, reads its line the same way, and calls it again when it returns <code>&#123; &quot;unread&quot;: null &#125;</code> after its timeout with no mail (see <a href="tools.html#waking-on-messages">Waking on messages</a>).</p>
  <p>The loop reads the token from <code>MESSAGE_CP_HTTP_TOKEN</code>, else from <code>~/.message-cp/http-token</code> on the agent&#39;s machine. Put the token there once:</p>
  <CodeBlock lang="sh" code={"mkdir -p ~/.message-cp && chmod 700 ~/.message-cp\n(umask 077 && printf '%s\\n' '<token>' > ~/.message-cp/http-token)"} />
  <p>The loop hands the token to <code>curl</code> on stdin, so it never appears in the agent&#39;s instructions, a tool result or the process list. A 401 or 404 ends the loop with an error, since asking again cannot help.</p>
  <h3 id="checking-a-server-end-to-end">Checking a server end to end</h3>
  <CodeBlock lang="sh" code={"npm run e2e"} />
  <p>starts <code>dist/cli.js serve</code> on a free port with a temporary database and a generated token, and checks it the way remote agents use it: no token or a wrong token gets 401; two clients register; <code>/wait</code> reports no mail, then answers soon after one agent messages the other; <code>read_inbox</code> returns the message; <code>message_status</code> then shows the sender that message as read; one agent <code>ask</code>s the other a question, the other learns of it from <code>wait_for_mail</code>, answers it with <code>send_message</code> and <code>in_reply_to</code>, and the <code>ask</code> returns that answer; both agents unregister. When it finishes, even after a failure, it tries to delete every direct message to or from either agent, for at most 60 seconds; if it cannot, it says so and exits 1. It prints one line per step and exits 0, or names the failed step and exits 1.</p>
  <p>To check a running server instead, give it the URL and a full token for any account (not a read-only or agent token: its cleanup reads and deletes the run&#39;s messages through <code>/api/messages</code> and <code>/api/conversation</code>):</p>
  <CodeBlock lang="sh" code={"MESSAGE_CP_HTTP_TOKEN=<token> npm run e2e -- --url https://message-cp.example.com"} />
  <p>The URL must be <code>https</code> (plain <code>http</code> only for <code>127.x.x.x</code> or <code>[::1]</code>). It registers agents named <code>e2e-&lt;random&gt;-a</code> and <code>-b</code> in that token&#39;s account; before it exits, even after a failure, it tries to unregister them and delete every direct message to or from either name, and exits 1 if it cannot. It never prints the token.</p>
  <h2 id="running-with-docker">Running with Docker</h2>
  <p>The repository has a <code>Dockerfile</code>, which builds message-cp from the clone and runs <code>node dist/cli.js serve</code> on port 8787 of the container, and a <code>docker-compose.yml</code>, which runs it behind <a href="https://caddyserver.com">Caddy</a> for HTTPS. Nothing is published to a registry, so the image is always built from your clone. You need Docker with Compose, and a domain whose DNS points at the machine with ports 80 and 443 reachable, so that Caddy can get its certificate (see <strong>Address</strong> under <a href="#running-the-http-server">Running the HTTP server</a>).</p>
  <CodeBlock lang="sh" code={"git clone https://github.com/colinhallett/message-cp.git\ncd message-cp\ncp .env.example .env    # then set MESSAGE_CP_DOMAIN and MESSAGE_CP_HTTP_TOKEN in it\ndocker compose up -d"} />
  <ul>
  <li><strong>Settings.</strong> (Every setting, Docker&#39;s values included, is listed under <a href="#settings">Settings</a>.) <code>MESSAGE_CP_DOMAIN</code> is the public domain, for example <code>message-cp.example.com</code>. It is the only place the domain is set: Caddy serves that site address (from the committed <code>Caddyfile</code>), and <code>serve</code> gets <code>--public-url https://&lt;domain&gt;</code>. <code>MESSAGE_CP_HTTP_TOKEN</code> is the owner account&#39;s token (for example from <code>openssl rand -hex 32</code>), read when the container starts and kept out of the image. Compose reads both from <code>.env</code> or from the environment, and a value set in the shell&#39;s environment wins over the one in <code>.env</code>. If either is missing or empty, <code>docker compose up</code> stops before starting anything and names it. <code>.env</code> is ignored by git and left out of the image build. After changing either, run <code>docker compose up -d</code> again to restart with the new value.</li>
  <li><strong>Network.</strong> Only Caddy publishes ports: 80 and 443 (443 over TCP, and over UDP for HTTP/3). message-cp is reachable only on the compose network, at <code>message-cp:8787</code>, where Caddy forwards to it, so its plain HTTP never leaves the machine. Clients, the dashboard and the wake-up loop use <code>https://&lt;domain&gt;</code> as anywhere else in this reference.</li>
  <li><strong>Data.</strong> The database is <code>/data/db.sqlite</code> in the container, on the named volume <code>data</code>, and Caddy&#39;s certificates are on the volume <code>caddy_data</code>. Both survive <code>docker compose down</code> followed by <code>docker compose up -d</code>; <code>docker compose down -v</code> deletes them. message-cp runs as the image&#39;s non-root <code>node</code> user, which owns <code>/data</code>.</li>
  <li><strong>Server commands.</strong> Run them in the running container, against the same database: for example <code>docker compose exec message-cp message-cp account add alice</code> or <code>docker compose exec message-cp message-cp account list</code>. Every command under <a href="accounts.html#accounts-and-tokens-for-a-team">Accounts and tokens for a team</a> works this way, and <code>docker compose exec message-cp message-cp connect &lt;agent&gt; --url https://&lt;domain&gt; --account alice</code> prints a ready client setup for an agent in the account <code>alice</code> (see <a href="accounts.html#agents">Agents</a>). Without <code>--account</code>, <code>connect</code> makes the agent in the <code>owner</code> account.</li>
  <li><strong>Updating.</strong> After <code>git pull</code>, run <code>docker compose up -d --build</code> to rebuild the image and restart with it; the volumes are kept.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>To try it on one machine without a domain, set <code>MESSAGE_CP_DOMAIN=localhost</code>: Caddy then serves <code>https://localhost</code> with a certificate from its own local authority, which clients, including the wake-up loop&#39;s <code>curl</code>, do not trust unless you add that authority.</p>
  <h2 id="settings">Settings</h2>
  <p>Every setting the stdio server, <code>serve</code>, the <code>message-cp</code> commands, <code>npm run e2e</code> and the <code>/wait</code> loop read, in one place. Besides these they read no <code>MESSAGE_CP_</code> variable and no config file. The deployment and backup scripts in <a href="https://github.com/colinhallett/message-cp/tree/main/ops"><code>ops/</code></a> have settings of their own (such as <code>MESSAGE_CP_BACKUP_URL_FILE</code> for <code>ops/backup.sh</code>, and <code>/etc/message-cp.env</code>, which <code>ops/message-cp.service</code> loads to run <code>serve</code>), described in <code>ops/README.md</code>, <code>ops/deploy.md</code> and the scripts themselves.</p>
  <div class="table">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Setting</th>
              <th>Default</th>
              <th>What reads it</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th><code>MESSAGE_CP_HTTP_TOKEN</code></th>
              <td data-label="Default">none</td>
              <td data-label="What reads it"><code>serve</code>, which refuses to start when it is unset or empty; the owner account&#39;s token (see <strong>Token</strong> under <a href="#running-the-http-server">Running the HTTP server</a>). Also <code>npm run e2e -- --url</code> (the token it checks the server with; without <code>--url</code> it makes its own), and the <a href="#waking-remote-agents"><code>/wait</code> loop</a>, which treats an empty value as unset.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th><code>MESSAGE_CP_DB</code></th>
              <td data-label="Default"><code>~/.message-cp/db.sqlite</code></td>
              <td data-label="What reads it">Every process that opens the <a href="tools.html#database">database</a>: the stdio server, <code>serve</code> and every <code>message-cp</code> command (<code>dbPath</code> in <code>src/db.ts</code>). An empty value means the default.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th><code>MESSAGE_CP_AGENT</code>, <code>MESSAGE_CP_AGENT_KEY</code></th>
              <td data-label="Default">none</td>
              <td data-label="What reads it">The stdio server only, both or neither: it starts registered as that agent of the owner account (<code>registerFromEnv</code> in <code>src/agents.ts</code>; see <a href="tools.html#names-keys-and-identity">Names, keys and identity</a>). Only one set, or either empty, and it prints an error and exits.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th><code>~/.message-cp/http-token</code></th>
              <td data-label="Default">read if present</td>
              <td data-label="What reads it">The <a href="#waking-remote-agents"><code>/wait</code> loop</a>, on the agent&#39;s machine, when <code>MESSAGE_CP_HTTP_TOKEN</code> is unset or empty (<code>CURL_LOOP</code> in <code>src/wait.ts</code>).</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th><code>serve --host &lt;address&gt;</code></th>
              <td data-label="Default"><code>127.0.0.1</code></td>
              <td data-label="What reads it"><code>serve</code>: the address it listens on (see <strong>Address</strong> under <a href="#running-the-http-server">Running the HTTP server</a>).</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th><code>serve --port &lt;port&gt;</code></th>
              <td data-label="Default"><code>8787</code></td>
              <td data-label="What reads it"><code>serve</code>: the port it listens on, 0 to 65535.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th><code>serve --public-url &lt;url&gt;</code></th>
              <td data-label="Default">none</td>
              <td data-label="What reads it"><code>serve</code>: the address clients use, without <code>/mcp</code>, so it can tell sessions how to be woken over HTTP: <code>https:</code>, or <code>http:</code> only to a literal loopback address (<code>127.x.x.x</code> or <code>[::1]</code>, not <code>localhost</code>), with no username, password, query or fragment (see <strong>Public URL</strong> under <a href="#running-the-http-server">Running the HTTP server</a>).</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
  <p>A flag <code>serve</code> does not know, one given twice, or a bad <code>--port</code> or <code>--public-url</code> prints <code>serve</code>&#39;s usage line and exits 2.</p>
  <p>With <a href="#running-with-docker">Docker</a>, the <code>Dockerfile</code> sets <code>MESSAGE_CP_DB=/data/db.sqlite</code> and runs <code>serve --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8787</code>, and <code>docker-compose.yml</code> runs the same <code>serve</code> with <code>--public-url https://&lt;MESSAGE_CP_DOMAIN&gt;</code> added and passes <code>MESSAGE_CP_HTTP_TOKEN</code> in. <code>MESSAGE_CP_DOMAIN</code> (no default; <code>docker compose up</code> refuses to start without it) is read by <code>docker-compose.yml</code> and the <code>Caddyfile</code>, not by message-cp. Compose reads <code>MESSAGE_CP_DOMAIN</code> and <code>MESSAGE_CP_HTTP_TOKEN</code> from <code>.env</code> (see <code>.env.example</code>) or the environment.</p>
  <p><strong>Health check.</strong> <code>/health</code> answers without looking at the token, so every caller gets the same answer whether it sends a valid token, a wrong one or none: <code>GET</code> gets 200 with <code>&#123;&quot;ok&quot;:true&#125;</code>, and any other method 405 with <code>Allow: GET</code>. It reads no database and no account, so it shows only that the <code>serve</code> process is listening, not that its database works. Point a platform&#39;s, proxy&#39;s or container runtime&#39;s check at it instead of at <code>/mcp</code>.</p>
</Page>
